                Preface

Proverbs 4:7 says wisdom is supreme; therefore get wisdom. Though it
costs all you have, get understanding (NIV). The Word of God is
unequivocal about the fact that without wisdom, one is hopeless, no
matter what else one has. In everything God does, He operates with
wisdom. Our revelation of God is not complete until we emphasise the
wisdom dimension of Him.

The first thing that comes to mind when the average person thinks
about God is how powerful He is, to do all He does. Truly, God is
great in power and He desires His people to know and reveal His
power. Have you ever wondered how wise God must be to do all He
does? This tendency to lean towards celebrating power over wisdom is
not because God has demonstrated more of His power than His
wisdom, rather it is because humans by default, seem to attach more
importance to the demonstration of power other than that of wisdom.
Thus, if a person divides a sea supernaturally, passes through and
closes it behind himself, leaving others behind, humans would for ever
look at such a person in awe. However, if another taps into innovation
and creativity to design and construct a bridge, opening a pathway for
others to cross after him, he may not receive as much admiration as the
first person even though what the second person has done is more
beneficial to the people and enduring than the first.
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

This is to say that we must learn to place high value and honour for the
demonstration of wisdom.

Oftentimes, power manifests by breaking natural laws, while wisdom
manifests by riding on natural occurrences. However, we must know
that both wisdom and power are the greatness of God in operation.
Power and wisdom are both divine attributes that complement each
other in the operations of God and as a child of God, you can and
should have both. Thus, Jeremiah 10:12 says, God has made the earth
by His power, He has established the world by His wisdom and has
stretched out the heavens at His discretion (NKJV). God made the
universe as a display of both His wisdom and power. Thus, Christ is
revealed as both the power of God and the Wisdom of God (1
Corinthians 1:24).

This book is a compendium of different nuggets of wisdom gleaned
from the principles of God revealed through His Word and His
operations.
                                 CHAPTER 1

     THE SEVEN STREAMS OF WISDOM

Wisdom is multi-dimensional. It is possible to manifest wisdom in one
area of life and display foolishness in another. Routinely take a look at
the area of life where you struggle the most and you will have a good
idea of the area where you are deficient in wisdom. A deficiency of
wisdom in an area is not a bad situation, it is not seeking wisdom in
one’s area of lack that is bad. This is the reason the scripture says if any
of you lacks wisdom, he should ask God, who gives generously to all
without finding fault, and it will be given to him (James 1:5). The worst
wisdom problem is not foolishness; it is lacking wisdom and not
seeking it. This is mostly due to pride or total oblivion of a higher
reality and it is the reason Proverbs 26:12 says, There is more hope for
fools than for people who think they are wise.

Generally, for the purpose of this book, we have identified seven
streams of wisdom. We chose seven streams because there always seems
to be something remarkable about giving seven points on something
that amazes people. The truth is that there are seven variants of words
in the Hebrew rendition of the Book of Proverb that are translated to
mean wisdom; understanding, knowledge, discretion and other related
words that show acumen. Proverbs 9:1 says, Wisdom has built her house;
she has carved its seven columns. There are seven operations of the Spirit
of God (Isaiah 11:2), and the Book of Revelation is replete with seven
churches, seven candlesticks, seven stars, seven seals, seven trumpets,
seven eyes and seven other things.

                                                               1
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

So, seven is the number of perfection. Permit me to postulate that
perfect wisdom must cover the seven areas below:

  1. Spiritual Wisdom: This is wisdom in the Word of God. It is
     absolute wisdom and understanding of spiritual laws. Spiritual
     wisdom makes one know what to do in the realm of the spirit to
     produce desired results in the natural. Spiritual wisdom is reflected
     in how well you know God and His principles. This is portrayed in
     Eph. 1:17, That the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory,
     may give unto you the spirit of wisdom and revelation in the
     knowledge of him, and Col 1: 9 which says, ‘Asking God, the glorious
     Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, to give you spiritual wisdom and
     insight so that you might grow in your knowledge of God’.

  2. Character Wisdom: Conduct and behaviour could be a valid proof
     of wisdom or absence of it. The attitude, disposition, and approach
     of a wise person is different from that of a fool. Respect, manner of
     speech, courtesy, honour for people, diligence, attitude to work, etc.
     can reflect the level of wisdom a person has and vice versa. In all
     cultures, a person who behaves well is seen as wise and the one who
     behaves badly as foolish. This is because life itself is structured in a
     way that its laws promote and favour people who have good
     character while penalising those with bad character. Bad conduct is
     self-destructive and moral uprightness is the way of wisdom. This is
     different from spiritual wisdom because there are people who are
     not ‘born again’ or even ‘spiritual’ but have good character and
     they make progress in life.

  3. Relational Wisdom: Managing and navigating relationships
     requires the skill of wisdom, very few people know that. Relational
     wisdom is simply having people skills. Lack of wisdom on how to
     manage relationships can lead to misuse and loss of vital
     relationships in one’s life from spousal to familiar relationships, to
     business relationships and to general relationships.

                                                               2
                                     THE SEVEN STREAMS OF WISDOM

 The way a person manages relationships with people is oftentimes a
measure of a dimension of wisdom in him or her.

      4. Practical/Pragmatic Wisdom: This is the dimension of wisdom
that is displayed in knowing how to manage affairs, make astute
decisions and good judgement in everyday life especially in practical
matters. Decisions like the ideal location to stay, the most suitable
transportation for a journey, best terms in negotiation of a deal, where,
when and how to make purchases, whether you should pay someone
upfront before delivering your goods or not, and lots of other decisions
demand practical discretion. Another word that describes pragmatic
wisdom is “street smartness”. Some people do not understand the
importance of practical application of street wisdom to making
decisions in everyday matters. Prov. 1:20-21 says, Wisdom shouts in the
streets for a hearing. She calls out to the crowds along Main Street, and to
the judges in their courts, and to everyone in all the land (TLB). Wisdom
is found in the streets! Lack of practical street wisdom is the reason
many naïve believers are conned and outsmarted by ‘cunning’ people
every day. Lack of street wisdom in Nigerian parlance is called ‘dulling
yourself”. ‘Dulling yourself’ is not spirituality. Practical wisdom is not
imparted by religiosity or spirituality, it is as a result of sound exposure,
calculated reason and sound understanding of the psychology of people
and your environment. Practical wisdom is essential.

      5. Learning or Academic Wisdom: Excellence in scholarly output is
a measure of wisdom. It is clearly shown in scriptures that knowledge
and understanding of ideas, concepts and books, good writing, reading,
communication skills as well as sound analytical and cognitive
reasoning, are a measure of wisdom. Dan. 1:17 shows how God gave
four young men an unusual aptitude for understanding every aspect of
literature and wisdom. And God gave Daniel the special ability to interpret
the meanings of visions and dreams. Good academic and professional
performance and prolific intellectual productivity are measures of
wisdom.

                                                               3
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

      6. Entrepreneurial or Business Wisdom: Enterprise and ability to
build a profitable system is a measure of wisdom. Most believers have
not come to terms with the importance of business wisdom; the ability
to make the best financial and profit-oriented decision for continuity
and sustainability of an enterprise. The wisdom of buying and selling,
capitalizing on opportunities, building wealth and generating income,
dealing with people with shrewd sense is called business wisdom.
Business wisdom, most times, is practical wisdom in the business scene.
It is not always developed in business school. According to 1 Kings
10:4-5, And when the queen of Sheba had seen all the wisdom of Solomon,
the house that he had built, 5 the food on his table, the seating of his
servants, the service of his waiters and their apparel, his cupbearers, and
his entryway by which he went up to the house of the Lord, there was no
more spirit in her. Solomon manifested business and organisational
wisdom in addition to all the other streams of wisdom that he had.

      7. Skill/Technical Wisdom: There is a dimension of wisdom that
shows itself as creativity, skill and ability to do things with aptitude and
proficiency. Wisdom in that area of skill is usually as a result of God
given grace and love for that field.

Ex 31:1-6 says, The Lord also said to Moses, "See, I have appointed
Bezalel (son of Uri, and grandson of Hur, of the tribe of Judah), and have
filled him with the Spirit of God, giving him great wisdom, ability, and
skill in constructing the Tabernacle and everything it contains. He is
highly capable as an artistic designer of objects made of gold, silver, and
bronze. He is skilled, too, as a jeweler and in carving wood. "And I have
appointed Oholiab (son of Ahisamach of the tribe of Dan) to be his
assistant; moreover, I have given special skill to all who are known as
experts, so that they can make all the things I have instructed you to
make. (TLB). Tech savviness, musical dexterity, unmatched sport
prowess, artistic finesse and mastery, and technical knack are often
expressions of wisdom in a specific area.

                                                               4
                                     THE SEVEN STREAMS OF WISDOM

This wisdom is oftentimes God-given and natural but could also be
developed. Even when natural, it still needs to be cultivated.

