                                 CHAPTER 3

   THE WISDOM OF SELF EVALUATION

Living an unappraised life is great folly. Periodic evaluation and routine
check-up of every area of your life is great wisdom. Many people do
things repeatedly without even pausing to check whether they are
getting the intended results from doing those things. It is commonly said
that doing the same thing repeatedly and expecting a different result is
folly. Well, living an unappraised life is what makes people continue
doing things without reconsideration.

Every time you are not living your desired life or getting the desired
outcome, take a look at your life and activities you engage in and find
out what is not been done rightly. Don’t be quick to allocate blame for
non-working areas of your life to other people, situation, national
problems, enemies and devils. Those external factors affect everybody
and some are still getting results despite these factors. So, what do you
need to change or do differently? What do you need to learn? Periodic
self-evaluation is a necessity for a successful life. Retreats and vacations
should not only be periods for rest, prayers or family get-together, they
should also be time to be alone and review your life, years and destiny ,
to check what is working and what is not working, what needs to be
sustained and what needs to be changed.

2Cor.13.5 says, Examine and test and evaluate your own selves to see
whether you are holding to your faith and showing the proper fruits of it.
Test and prove yourselves [not Christ].

                                                               9
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

 Do you not yourselves realize and know [thoroughly by an ever-
increasing experience] that Jesus Christ is in you—unless you are
[counterfeits] disapproved on trial and rejected? (AMP). The Message
Version puts it this way; test yourselves to make sure you are solid in the
faith. Don't drift along taking everything for granted. Give yourselves
regular checkups. You need firsthand evidence, not mere hearsay, that
Jesus Christ is in you. Test it out. If you fail the test, do something about
it. (MSB)

In the Creation Account in Gen 1, after every day of creation, it is
written … ‘and God saw what He created that it was good’. This shows
that God daily appraised His output at creation and marked each day
satisfactory. Self-appraisal should be an everyday task. At the end of
the day, check how the day went, where you wasted time, things you
wrote you were going to do and you did not do, things you should not
have done or said that you did say and do, then make decisions to
improve on your short comings.

Apart from daily self-evaluation, there should also be times when you
take time off from the hustle-bustle of life just for the purpose of a more
robust and intensive self-appraisal. In this period, you take a look at the
life goals that you have set before now, inspect them and see where you
have performed poorly in all honesty, then make those areas a priority
going forward. An unexamined life is a life pre-disposable to error.
Engage the Wisdom of Self-Examination today.

