                                 CHAPTER 6

             THE WISDOM OF SOLITUDE

Though it may not be a conventional knowledge, a secret strategy that
every great and visionary person should engage is the Wisdom of
Solitude - being alone and going alone when occasion demands. Have
you noticed that most of the activities that involve the highest level of
personal productivity need a level of solitude for appropriate focus?
Prayer, studying, practising a skill, meditating, writing and a host of
other exercises that are important for life progress, need one to be alone
to do them effectively. Even when such activities are to be done publicly
with others, it is how well each individual have mastered them privately
that determines how well they do them publicly.

What a person does with their solitary time is often a direct reflection of
the person’s confidence, vision, character and productivity. A leader,
most especially, must learn to take time to be alone to think, plan and
work creatively. Every leader knows that eagles do not fly in a flock;
they soar solo: It is sparrows, chicks and ducks that always need
company before they can have the confidence to move. The sad truth is
that some people are such bad company that they cannot even be their
own company. they always need someone else’s ‘better company’. A
leader cannot afford to live with a flock mentality - doing things with
everyone else and doing only what is done by everyone else.
It is important to know when to be with the crowd and when to retreat
from the crowd.

                                                              21
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

This is because there are some things that one will not have access to
unless one takes off time to ‘download’ them in the secret place. Matt
10:27 says, What I tell you in the dark, speak in the daylight; what is
whispered in your ear, proclaim from the roofs. This shows that the
things that will proclaim you on the rooftops of life are the things you
heard or did privately. Luke 8: 17 also reiterates, For nothing is secret,
that shall not be made manifest; neither any thing hid, that shall not be
known and come abroad. The great things you are doing privately are
the things that have the ability to announce and make you manifest to
your world.

Part of the Wisdom of Solitude is making periodic retreats an integral
part of your life. There will always be perpetual demands on you as a
leader which will look like you can’t get away from them. However, the
truth is that if you are drained and burnout, all the people will leave you
and look for an alternative. This is the reason it is wise to periodically
retreat from the hustle and bustle of life, the pressures, demands of life
and the demands of the people to go and refuel your spirit, soul, body
and vision in the place of solitude. The rule of the thumb for the
mindset of retreating into solitude is to remember that “there is no one
that is so indispensable that the world will fall apart when he is
temporarily unavailable.”

It is also worthy of note that everyone that did great things in this life
had a season of their life when they willingly or without much choice
had to be in protracted solitude. In Exodus 3:1-4, there is the record of
Moses’ encounter with God that launched him into destiny. ‘Now
Moses was tending the flock of Jethro, his father-in-law, the priest of
Midian, and he led the flock to the far side of the desert and came to
Horeb, the mountain of God. There the angel of the Lord appeared to him
in flames of fire from within a bush. Moses saw that though the bush was
on fire it did not burn up. So Moses thought, "I will go over and see this
strange sight — why the bush does not burn up."

                                                              22
                                          THE WISDOM OF SOLITUDE

When the Lord saw that he had gone over to look, God called to him from
within the bush, "Moses! Moses!”. The truth is that most encounters that
will change a person’s life dramatically and forever will be experienced
in the secret place. It is important to note that God does not only
encounter people in the open and in corporate meeting but solitude.
Truthfully, there are things that God only does to people on an
individual level. Many public encounters are to drive people into the
place of solitude where the work of God in their lives continues.

The story of Moses period of solitude did not start at the bushing bush,
it dates into his forty years of sheep rearing alone in the wilderness. He
was chosen by God for the great task of leading Israel out of bondage in
Egypt. He was a public figure in the palace limelight in Egypt and
wanted to achieve his destiny on the strength of that positioning.
However, he failed in the mission. It was after he was exiled and had
learned a lot through the practice of the wisdom of solitude that he was
ready for God’s commissioning. Great destinies are not prepared for in
the public but in the private incubator of solitude. This is the reason
after Moses started his life mission; he was already addicted to being
alone with God in His presence. He spent a cumulative of 80 days alone
with God on the mountain. What is birthed in the secret place of being
alone with God will also be sustained in the secret place of being alone
with God.

God will not give a person a crucial task unless the person is established
in the details of the assignment. These details and instructions are
received in the secret place where the leader and visionary communes
with the Spirit of God and writes the instructions of the vision(s). This
is expressed in Prov. 18:1 which says, through desire a man, having
separated himself, seeketh and intermeddleth with all wisdom

David had his share of encounters in solitude. In fact, the confidence he

                                                              23
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

had to face Goliath was developed from the victories he had won when
he fought in solitude. According to 1 Samuel 17:34-37, David said to
Saul, "Your servant has been keeping his father's sheep. When a lion or a
bear came and carried off a sheep from the flock, I went after it, struck it
and rescued the sheep from its mouth. When it turned on me, I seized it by
its hair, struck it and killed it. David built capacity for great exploits in
the secret and when it was time for him to manifest, he did so
seamlessly. Your public victory is determined by your secret diligence.
Success is not attained in front of the crowd but in the private place of
preparation. The truth is that the higher one progresses through life and
in leadership, the more difficult it becomes to create time off the
legitimate demands of life to seek God, develop one’s self and grow. It is
only those that are already addicted to the secret place that keep
making out time to be alone with God and to grow irrespective of how
busy the demands of life become.

The necessity of solitude for greatness seems to be consistent with
scriptures. It is one of God’s secret strategies for forging his choice
instruments. God would isolate them, work on them, build and equip
them before He opens the door of manifestation for them. If you seem
to be in a season when you are alone with God and the public lights are
not on you, then you should know you are in good company.

When Paul was called, he first wanted to start public manifestation
immediately but he met resistance. In fact, even without resistance, he
might not have had the level of success he eventually had. This is
because the resistance and persecution he experienced made him retreat
to Arabia and later to Damascus. He also spent some time in his
hometown Tarsus and later in Antioch where he spent years praying
and growing alongside some other people (Acts 13:2). It was after many
years of his conversion that he was eventually commissioned by God
into the ministry.

                                                              24
                                          THE WISDOM OF SOLITUDE

No one succeeds in a great venture until they have paid their dues in
ample time of growth and development in the season of oblivion.

It is noteworthy that the final announcement of a person who has been
vetted in solitude is completely within God’s sovereignty and
