                                 CHAPTER 10

             THE WISDOM OF GROWTH

Growth is one of the most powerful mysteries of life. One of the wisest
decisions one can make is to be committed to continual growth in every
area of life. There are untold potentials and opportunities available to
anyone who keeps growing. The egg in the womb of a woman is
fertilised and with growth, it becomes a sophisticated human being who
manufactures jet engines, designs complex computer systems and sits as
executive over a board of conglomerates, multinationals and national
organisations. Growth is the difference between little and great. Thus,
Matt 13: 31 explains that the Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed
which a man took and sowed in his field, which is indeed the least of all the
seeds; but when it is grown it is greater than the herbs and becomes a tree,
so that the birds of the air come and nest in its branches. (NKJV).

The Kingdom way is starting small and growing big. Growth is being
ever-progressive: it must be seen as a journey and not a destination.
Don’t ever stop growing. When people think they have arrived and do
not strive for growth , they begin to lose worth and relevance. Growth
makes you magnetic and keeps you relevant. One way to keep growing
is to keep learning and keep daring new things that stand the chance of
moving you forward.

However, it is important to note that one’s environment determines and

                                                              44
                                           THE WISDOM OF GROWTH

affects the chances of growth. Fishes grow in water and die on land. A
lion reigns supreme in the jungle and dies in water. An eagle dominates
in the sky but struggles in a cage. You do not stand an equal chance of
thriving in all environments.
Your environment which describes the culture, practices, location,
facilities, resources, organisation and people around you, determines
your growth. It is wise to change environment if where you are is not
supportive of growth.

Mark 4: 31 - 33 says, “The kingdom of God is as if a man should scatter
seed on the ground, and should sleep by night and rise by day, and the seed
should sprout and grow, he himself does not know how. For the earth
yields crops by itself: first the blade, then the head, after that the full grain
in the head. But when the grain ripens, immediately he puts in the sickle,
because the harvest has come.” The sower sows the seed and with the
right systems in place, it grows till it produces massive fruits. The earth,
by a divine ordinance, is commanded to make things grow whenever the
right systems are in place.

Another important growth system is your routines. Routines are things
you do consistently and periodically that sharpen the outcome of your
life. There are routines that produce growth and routines that prevents
growth. When you see a child daily, you do not notice he/she is
growing. However, after a while, growth is noticeable on sight.
Routines are very powerful system for growth A beginner in learning a
skill becomes a master by the routine of daily rehearsal, practice and
workouts. Growth produced by routines may be unnoticeable at first as
daily routines seem not to be making much difference until one day
when remarkable growth becomes obvious. Daily routines of growth
include daily extensive time of prayer, solitary

                                                              45
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

meditation, study, reading and writing, rehearsal and practice, exercise
and workouts, diligent repeated exertion at a task, learning and
training, productivity, value addition and selling.

Therefore, the wisdom of growth entails knowing what to order aright
in your life, environment, attitudes, routines and systems that growth
becomes a spontaneous effect of your daily life. Success comes on its
own as the system for growth is put in place.

Growth must be holistic; growing in an aspect of life and leaving out
another produces a handicapped Growth must be intentional and
strategic for it to be beneficial. The first step in growing sustainably is to
understand what holistic growth entails because it is very possible to
grow in an area of life and not grow in another. A vital understanding
in growth is to know the vital areas of life-growth. Growing in an area
of life and not growing in another produces an haphazard life-structure
that eventually results in life-malfunction and sometimes, life-collapse.

The multi-dimensional nature of growth is described in the
developments of Samuel who was said to ‘grow in stature, in favour
with the Lord and in favour with men’ and, Jesus who ‘grew, and waxed
strong in spirit, was filled with wisdom and the grace of God was upon
him .’
The different dimensions to growth include;
(i) Spiritual Growth in one’s relationship with God.
(ii) Mental Growth in knowledge, understanding and intellectual
development.
(iii) Emotional Growth in self-mastery, character and conduct.
(iv) Financial Growth in developing additional streams of income

                                                              46
                                           THE WISDOM OF GROWTH

and expanding existing ones.
(v) Growth in skills, proficiency, certifications, training, and expertise –
becoming more proficient in existing skills and/or developing new ones.
(vi) Relational and Social Growth; in strengthening beneficial
relationships, making new advantageous connections and truncating
exiting toxic relationships.
(vii) Material and Structural Growth in acquisition of more possession,
generation of greater results, organizational expansion and numerical
addition to what one does.
(viii) Physical, Biological and Chronological Growth which comes as a
result of chronological passage of time.
Please note that; physical/biological growth is the only dimension of
growth that is automatic. Even at that, the right environment like good
food, shelter and air have to be in place. Every other dimension of
growth needs to be intentionally engaged. The only thing that grows in
many people’s lives is their chronological age. Such people are not
growing up, they are merely growing old.

