            THE WISDOM OF LEARNING

‘Learning is synonymous to living’; this statement cannot be over-
emphasised. Meaning, ‘to live is to learn; and to stop learning, is to stop
living’. Learning is the noble factor that defines one's quality of life.
Societally, a learned person has potential for a higher quality of life and
is considered and distinguished than his/her unlearned peers. This
implies that, one indispensable virtue that makes for greatness in life is
learning; the enthusiasm and the guided passion to go for knowledge
and acquire it. Learning is the humility to consider one’s self as having
little and being open to receive more. It is one's discontentment with the
current level of one's exposure, and then seeking to be more enlightened.
Learning begins with the acknowledgement of one's insufficiencies and
inadequacies, then subjecting oneself to impartation and equipping of
knowledge.

The Bible in Prov. 1:5 says: "A wise man will hear, and will increase
learning; and a man of understanding shall attain unto wise counsels."
This is to say that a wise man is one who gives his ears to hearing, so as
to increase his learning. The scripture here, measures one's wisdom by
one's openness to listen and learn. Learning is not a passive experience;
it is an intentional act of yielding and receiving. In Acts 7:22, the Bible
says: "And Moses was learned in all the wisdom of the Egyptians, and was
mighty in words and in deeds." This scripture here as well, measures
one's might by learning.

