                                                              76
                                 CHAPTER 19

         THE WISDOM OF CONSISTENCY

Consistency is one proof of greatness. It takes greatness to execute a
commendable project but it takes ‘greater greatness’ to consistently
deliver on it. When you meet someone who has consistently lived up to
repeated outstanding demand, expectation, and task or feat for a long
span of time, you have met a great person.
Many people do commendable things - win a challenge, organise an
event, exert at worthy disciplines, or carry out notable undertakings
here and there, but the dilemma of success is whether they will do it
again and for how long they will be able to sustain it. The truth is that
the magnitude of what we do is, often times, not as important as how
many times we do it. Truly, one will go further by consistently taking
incremental steps than mustering all one’s strength for a jump that
leaves one exhausted and gasping for breath. A mistake people always
make is trying to take one big step which they cannot sustain instead of
consistently taking repeated steps in the same direction over a long
period of time.

Organisations grow by building and widening their operation base
through incremental addition rather than by daring doing over-sized
projects or events that they can’t run consistently. Start-up businesses
and ministries, show artists, aspiring musicians and public speakers, and
some other categories of people who provide value/service through
gathering people for events often make the mistake of rating magnitude

                                                              77
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

over consistency. Some have gone to the extent of pushing themselves
beyond limits or even incur my debt to host an event in an attempt to
gather an enormous number people. The starter usually spends so much
to host an event and invite a ‘big name’ in his field, whose presence will
compel a crowd. However, if such an event cannot be consistently
sustained what usually happens is that the big event creates a big
sensation and a pseudo picture of growth which returns to status quo
immediately the event is over. If the intention is to generate a one-time
profit on ticket sale or just make an impression that makes sense, but if
it is to sustainably build a platform, consistency is the name of the
game.

Of course, this is not to say that big events are not valuable. The truth is
that such events are of great use when there is a platform that runs
consistently which the event will feed, and into which the proceeds from
the big event will be harvested. Everyone who has ever built a working
platform - business, ministry or charity, knows that many times, the
proceeds of growth from a mega event, no matter how minimal could be
worthwhile as long as it adds value to an existing consistent platform
one is building. It is wise not to measure success merely by the execution
of a mega project but by its addition spiritually, numerically,
intellectually or otherwise to the existing platform. Consistency allows
growth to be measurable.
James 1:25 says but he who looks into the perfect law of liberty AND
CONTINUES IN IT, and is not a forgetful hearer but a doer of the work,
this one will be blessed in what he does (NKJV). Even beneficial spiritual,
intellectual and character disciplines only thrive on the basis of
consistency. An emergency approach to such disciplines will not give
the desired results. There are people who declare unreasonably long
days of marathon fasting and prayers because they have problems, not

                                                              78
                                       THE WISDOM OF CONSISTENCY

knowing that they would experience more spiritual impact and growth,
and probably have avoided the problem if they had developed a
consistent program of hours of regular prayers and fasting, and
consistent study over time. It is the same mind-set that makes students
crash read when examination has dawned upon them instead of
building a consistent reading culture over the term.
1 Timothy 4:16 says, Take heed unto thyself, and unto the doctrine;
continue in them: for in doing this thou shalt both save thyself, and them
that hear thee. It is in consistency and continuing to do the right things
that you bring profit to yourself and people around you. The
blessedness of any good practice is the consistency of doing it.
Consistency ascertains progress and results. It is very important to
factor consistency in our plans. Sometimes, it is wise not to initiate
things until you are sure you can sustain it consistently, Visions die
without consistency, even brilliant performances result in failure
without consistency. Consistency birth results and makes one
outstanding. Whatever beneficial thing you are doing, factor
consistency into the plan.

                                                              79
                                 CHAPTER 20

           THE WISDOM OF CHARACTER

From historical times, the world has seen the emergence of powerful
leaders, with brilliant minds and skillful hands across various spheres of
influence. These leaders were notable for their conquests, royalty,
inventions, philosophies, business strategies, political policies, creative
arts, military power, etc. They held the world in awe, for their indelible
impacts on the sands of time; men revered them as gods on earth. But,
sadly, in spite of their great achievements and successes, many of them
failed to secure their great names in the stronghold of good character,
and so, they were not worthy models.

Character is a virtue; the quality of being just and doing justice.
Character is one's strength to adhere to principles that make for
morality and integrity. The features of good character will define it
more explicitly; truthfulness, uprightness, straightforwardness,
authenticity, honesty, integrity, rectitude, temperance, humility,
courage, faithfulness, sincerity, perseverance, etc. These and many other
moral virtues are the indices to measure a person of good character.

The Bible instructs in Philippians 4:8 "Finally, brethren, whatsoever
things are true, whatsoever things are honest, whatsoever things are just,
whatsoever things are pure, whatsoever things are lovely, whatsoever
things are of good report; if there be any virtue, and if there be any praise,
think on these things.

                                                              80
                                        THE WISDOM OF CHARACTER

The Amplified version says "think on and weigh and take account of
these things [fix your minds on them]" That is, keep them in your mind,
meditate on them, let them become the underlying principles which defines
your actions and reactions.
The need to be a person of strong and upright character can never be
overemphasized, the Bible is full of stories of men whose lack of good
character cost them their birth rights, thrones, destines, fulfilments, and
even their lives. These men struggled to keep up with the quality of
being a person of good character, but they could not, and hence, their
stories ended tragically. Even the wisest man that ever lived, who
himself taught on the essence of good character in Proverbs 1:7a, "The
fear of the Lord is the beginning of knowledge" could not hold on to his
convictions till the end. This is what then gave rise to the notion of "no
one is perfect, we are all humans, after all". So, men began to justify
their wrongs with excuses of just being human, which may appear
understandable and consolable, but the reign of corruption continues to
extend across dispensations.

It is important to know that the wisest thing to do at any point is the
godliest thing. Whenever, you are at a crossroad of choice between
good and evil. Know that ‘good’ is the wise choice even if it looks stupid
as at the time. A life of character and moral uprightness is a life of
wisdom. Cutting corners, making unprincipled compromises and
bending standards even when they present better outcomes temporarily
will always cost more in the long run. Integrity, godliness and good
character are great wisdom and they are the foundation and sustaining
power of all other types of wisdom.

Jesus Christ, the All-Wise One came as the exact portrait of the
character which God expects His children to possess.

                                                              81
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

Jesus came to show us the possibilities of living a life of godliness, even
in a perverse world; and then gave us the nature of God, by which we
can become people of good character and live a godly lifestyle, pleasing
and acceptable to God. The Most Holy One is also the All Wise One -
this hits home the point that character is wisdom.

Believers in Christ Jesus are potentially natured to live a life of
godliness, which is the benchmark of character. We are without excuses,
for we have the nature of God which is godliness. This is possible
because of the indwelling of the Spirit of God in us, which when
nurtured, produces fruits of godliness. These fruits, doctrinally known
as the fruits of the Spirit are clearly outlined in Galatians 5:22-23 "But
the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness,
goodness, faith, meekness, temperance: against such there is no law."
Believers should become models of good character across various
sectors and strata. We should be able to say ‘No’ to fornication and
adultery, like Joseph, "how shall I do this and sin against God." We
should be able to imitate Daniel and say: " I will not defile myself with
the King's sumptuous meal". And like the three Hebrew men, "Let it be
known to you o King, we will not bow to your god". And also like Apostle
Paul, in the midst of affliction and persecution, "to live is Christ and to
die is gain." This wisdom of character will not just keep us upright in
our dealings with others, but will also secure our glorious destinies in
God. The world should come to us to know of this wisdom of character,
and by this, we can change the ungodly system of the world unto Christ-
Centeredness.

