                                                              83
                                 CHAPTER 21

            THE WISDOM OF HUMILITY

Humility is an essential virtue of character and wisdom in a Christian.
One ultimate measure of Christlikeness in a person is the level of
humility. Matt 11: 28 - 29 says, Come to me, all you who are weary and
burdened, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you and learn from
me, for I am gentle and humble in heart, and you will find rest for your
souls. For my yoke is easy and my burden is light. Among many things to
learn from Jesus Christ is His kind of humility.

Humility is knowing who you are, being secure and confident in
yourself at whatever phase of life you are, knowing fully well that God
is the one who made you all you are and being confident He is the one
who will make you all you still need to be, therefore, ascribing all the
glory and honor due to Him for anything you are or have. Pride is
undue preoccupation with oneself, opinions, ambitions, abilities,
accomplishments, position, or possessions. Many times, it manifests in
seeing others as inferior and expecting glory or recognition from others
for one’s self-perceived worth.

Prov. 16: 7 lists the things God hates and number one is a proud
countenance/heart. If sins were in hierarchies, pride would be rated as
number one ; after all, it is the oldest sin that gives birth to all other
sins. If virtues were also in hierarchies, humility would rate first.

                                                              84
                                          THE WISDOM OF HUMILITY

Sadly, humility has been grossly misconceived and misunderstood to
mean self-abasement, being cowardly and unconfident, or walking with
heads and backs bowed in timidity and subservience. Another
misconception in Christianity about being humble is that humility is a
noble art of denying your strength and being over-conscious of your
weaknesses. Humility is not living a low life with no aspiration for
greatness. Jesus, the Most Humble did not mince words about declaring
who He was and how great He was. When He told them that He was
the Son of God, the Messiah that was to come, the One greater than
Abraham, Solomon and all the prophets, they could not handle that
level of boldness.

It is wisdom to be humble, however make sure what you call humility is
not conferred upon you by the expectation of people but by God’s
definition as revealed in the life of Jesus.

Apostle Paul instructs in Phil. 2:3 "Let nothing be done through strife or
vainglory; but in lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than
themselves." A humble person knows who he is and what he is capable
of but still has high regards for one another, appreciating our own
strengths, knowing they are given by God and at the same time,
recognizing, appreciating and celebrating others for their uniqueness,
and we should forbear one another in love and faith. A humble person
is quick to transfer praise to others while a proud person has a
voracious appetite for human praise.

There are many verses of the Bible, stories and illustrations that show
God's intense displeasure towards pride and the fruits it bears. One of
such scriptures is 1 Pet. 5:5b "God resisteth the proud, and giveth grace
to the humble."

                                                              85
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

This is a sharp written verdict concerning God's primary judgement on
the proud. That, it is God Himself who stands in the way of the proud,
to impede his progress, for his end result will be to take the glory that
belongs to God, and God will not let it happen. Then, the other end of
the scripture explains how God empowers the humble unto progress
and advancement in what he does, for God knows that, it is all unto His
glory. In another scripture, it says that "God exalts the humble." (1Pet.
5:6) This and more shows how much God cherishes humility and the
humble, and he is not afraid to elevate them, for they are carriers of His
glory. Therefore, it is really wise to be humble.

The following are wisdom indices that measure humility in a person.

1. Teachability and willingness to learn: a humble person always
maintains a posture of being ready to learn even from people she might
be above. 1Cor. 8:2 paints this posture beautifully "And if any man
think that he knoweth anything, he knoweth nothing yet as he ought to
know." A humble person is always open to listen.

2. Submissiveness to Authority: A very key index of measuring humility
is a person’s relationship with authority. Proud people always have
problems with authorities. Humble people are able to be under
authority and submit and also use authority boldly and for the good of
others when in authority.

3. Relationship with correction: A proud man thinks so highly of himself,
he believes he knows how best to do what he does, and it will be an
insult to him if anyone finds a fault in it, then tries to correct him. But a
humble man is not so, being in a posture of teachability, he does what

                                                              86
                                          THE WISDOM OF HUMILITY

 he does and he's even open to know a better way of which it can be
done; so, he receives every correction with gratitude. A humble man can
be easily led out of an erroneous path, and led into a path wherein he
should go, just because he is ever willing to embrace corrections. But for
the proud man it is not so, for the Bible even declared it "Pride goeth
before destruction" (Prov. 16:18), for he gave no room for correction.
4. Meekness: the ability to be doing a lot, yet saying little. A humble
man is engrossed in doing more to get much more that he does not even
have the time to make a noise of himself. He is never under pressure to
show off his results so as to win accolades and respect. For such a man,
it is the benefactors of his impact that noise him abroad, he is never
arrogant nor puffed up by what he has amassed, for his heart is never in
those things. He is meek, gentle and noble, in speech and conduct.

                                                              87
                                 CHAPTER 22

             THE WISDOM OF HONOUR

Honour is the recognition, acknowledgement and reward of a person’s
value, contribution, role and worth. Honour, like gratitude, must be
expressed for it to be truly called honour. Honour is a thing of the heart
and like, infatuation, it cannot be hidden. So God described certain
people and said, these people come near to me with their mouth and
honour me with their lips, but their hearts are far from me (Isa 29:13,
Matt 15: 9, Mk 7:6). The truth is a person with sound judgement and
discretion can discern both honour and dishonour from a mile away.
Do not dishonour people and do not be comfortable with being
dishonoured. Dishonour is unprofitable both to the giver and the
recipient. Avoid dishonour from the giving and receiving side.

The uncomfortable truth is that for every incessant struggle and failure
in a life, there is a principle or person they are dishonouring. If a bad
situation refuses to change despite all odds, there is an element of
dishonour contributing to its perpetuation. I have seen poor people
being invited to buy a book or pay for a seminar, or sometimes, attend a
conference where a rich person wants to speak and they refuse. Some
say that ‘is he going to give me money there?’ That is gross dishonour
for all the knowledge, value and results that person has. The little
person that honours the great and overlooks their excesses to pursue all
they represent will soon be great. There are sick people that count it too
inconvenient to go and attend a meeting where a truly anointed servant

                                                              88
                                           THE WISDOM OF HONOUR

of God is ministering. Some sick people find it unnecessary to heed the
doctor’s advice. Check every area of your life where there is a challenge
and look up to the person around you or a principle that you have
neglected long enough, that might just be the solution. Going the extra
mile to seek a person or thing is a proof of honour. Some men climbed a
roof, tore it open and got their sick friend to Jesus when everywhere was
blocked and Jesus did not see it as violence but honour for him so he
healed the man. It amazes me how some people that have something to
receive or learn from a greater person behave as if the greater should be
the one seeking them. It is gross dishonour for the value that person has
to offer you. An evidence of honour is pursuit.

What one does not honour, one cannot have and even if one has it, he
will soon lose it. It is through honour you get access to greatness and
through honour one sustains greatness. In 1 Sam 2:30-31, God spoke to
some who displayed dishonour, "Therefore, the Lord, the God of Israel,
says: I promised that your branch of the tribe of Levi would always be my
priests. But I will honor those who honor me, and I will despise those who
think lightly of me’’. Dishonour is a sin - it is the twin brother of pride.
God hates them both and does not tolerate dishonourable people in His
exalted place.

It is important that you honour all men that you relate with. It is easier
for many to honour people above them but the true test of honour is
how you honour those who are below you. 1 Peter 2:17 says, Honour all
men. Love the brotherhood. Fear God. Honour the king. This shows there
is the honour you accord to all humans as a basic right and there is the
honour you accord to the king (leader) because he is above you.
Therefore, it is right to honour every person you come across whether
your superior, colleague or subordinate and there is an additional

                                                              89
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

honour you give to someone because they are your superior. So, a wise
person looks beyond the present state of people and discerns the value
to honour. Make honouring people a core value of your life as the Bible
also instructs in Rom. 12:10 "Love one another with brotherly affection.
Outdo one another in showing honor."(ESV).

If it is wisdom to honour, then it is foolishness to dishonour. Dishonour
is the lack of recognition, condescension or pursuit of a grace or value
that has the ability to add to you. To dishonour is to build a wall of
barrier between you and what the person has to add to you. Dishonour
is also sowing a seed, but a seed unto unproductivity, lack and
lowliness. Whatever is dishonoured depreciates and whatever one does
not honour cannot do one any good. Thus, nothing or no one can
benefit you if you dishonour them in your heart. This is the reason
many people are in a place and are not making headway there. You
cannot dishonour your pastor, spouse, parent or boss in your heart and
wonder why their presence is not benefiting you. Conversely, you
cannot be a sustainable blessing to someone or in a place you are
dishonoured. This is the reason it is wisdom to humbly withdraw from
places and people where you are treated dishonourably as it would be
mutually non-beneficial for such a relationship to continue. If I cannot
benefit you since you dishonour me and I also depreciate because I am
dishonoured, then both of us would lose in the relationship - it is better
I walk away.

The first level of honour should be unto God. Prov. 3:9-10 records,
