                                 CHAPTER 25

             THE WISDOM OF SECRECY

Sometimes, silence is the greatest expression of wisdom. A young boy
was shown a picture of his enviable future. He woke up from his sleep,
perplexed, he couldn't understand nor read meaning to this baffling
vision he saw. In his restlessness, he couldn't keep the door of his
mouth; his naivety and curiosity drove him to his elder brothers, he
narrated his dream to them, and they, being elderly could deduce that
his dream is supposed to be a prophecy of his future superiority over.
They hated him, envied and despised him. This young boy unwittingly
opened up himself to a life of loneliness, rejection and persecution
because he was void of wisdom at that time. (see Genesis 37:1-11) Very
few things are more foolish and more devastating in life than telling
things about yourself to a person(s) that you are not supposed to. The
Wisdom of Secrecy is an advanced course in the school of wisdom.

The Wisdom of Secrecy is the art of knowing what to say, when to say it
and to whom it is to be said to. This wisdom is so important to have
that the psalmist prayed earnestly to God in Psalms 141:3" LORD, set a
guard over my mouth; keep watch over the door to my lips". Many
glorious destinies have been aborted because of indiscretion in revealing
things to a person that shouldn't have known of it. In the Bible, Saul
went looking for his father’s missing donkey and the search led him to
Prophet Samuel’s house where he was told he would be the next king
and even anointed for it.

                                                             102
                                                  31 WISDOM KEYS

When he got back home and the people got wind that he had the
privilege to meet with the great Prophet Samuel, they were curious to
know what the man of God told him. In 1 Sam 10: 15 - 16, Saul’s uncle
said, tell me what Samuel said to you. Saul replied, ‘He assured us that
the donkeys had been found.’’ But he did not tell his uncle what Samuel
had said about the kingship (NIV). What a great display of wisdom! He
was given some information by the Prophet; he knew which was for
public consumption, and which was for his private keep. This wisdom
secured him till he ascended the throne and the prophecy was fulfilled.
He kept details of his revealed future even from some people because he
was wise enough to discern that in this case, "a man's enemies may be
the men of his own house."

Lack of the wisdom of secrecy opens one up to wrong counsel. Do you
know that most of the people who gave you wrong counsel were people
that had no business being told about the situation actually? This was
the plight of Rehoboam, the Son of Solomon a young king in the Bible.
It is amazing how children of very wise people could be very foolish. So
make sure you teach people around you wisdom and don’t assume it
will automatically rub off on them. He consulted two categories of
people on an issue but he couldn't discern the right counsel to heed and
whose counsel to avoid. He followed wrong counsel from his friends
and destroyed the kingdom his fathers built for years (See 1 Kings 12:8-
16). He should not have discussed the issue with his friends.

It is not friendship or courtesy that dictates who you tell things about
yourself, it is discretion.

In 1 Kings 13, a young prophet was given a public message to go and
deliver in a city and a private instruction not to eat in the city or come

                                                             103
                                           THE WISDOM OF SECRECY

back the same route he went. He delivered both the public message and
the private instruction to everyone. It was the details of the private
instruction that the older prophet that masterminded his death used to
destroy him. So, this implies that biological age or cultural respect
should not be the primary reason why you should give people the details
of your destiny and access to counsel you, but rather, you should be led
by the Spirit of God and be discerning by the Spirit of Wisdom to know
who deserves and who does not deserve to know things about you. It is
not everyone that has the right counsel for your specific situation. Thus,
you have to discern through wisdom and prayer who God wants you to
talk to in a particular situation.

The Lord Jesus Christ always knew what about Him was for private
keep and for public consumption. So in several places in the Bible, he
would do or say things, even good things, and would tell his disciple to
make sure no one knows about it. In some cases, he will take the three
most trusted out of all his disciples and allow only them to see or hear
certain things about him. There are many other occasions in the Bible
where Jesus would heal privately and instruct the healed not to go
around telling everyone what he has done. He did this to teach them a
powerful lesson of being discreet about what God was doing in their
lives per season. While it is good to share testimonies of God's
goodness, it is wise to discern what to share and what to keep to
yourself. It is not every breakthrough you have that is meant for the
social media.
The Wisdom of Secrecy is a necessary virtue for preservation of destiny.

